# Engineering Task Board

Kanban board: PostgreSQL + React + ONE backend (.NET, Python, or Java) behind a
shared REST contract. Domain is small; keep layering and the contract honest.

## Architecture — do not violate

| Layer | Allowed | Never |
|-------|---------|-------|
| Controller / Router | HTTP <-> domain, map errors to status codes | Business rules, SQL |
| Service | Validation, business rules | HTTP concerns, SQL |
| Repository | ALL database access | Validation, HTTP |

React: `components/` (presentational) → `pages/` (state + fetching) → `services/` (HTTP only).

## Hard rules

- Do not read `.env`, `.env.local`, or other `.env.*` (except `.env.example`), or secrets. Use the framework config system.
- Schema is owned once in `database/schema.sql`. Do not add EF migrations, `Base.metadata.create_all()`, or `ddl-auto` other than `none`.
- `created_at` / `updated_at` are set by the database, never the client.
- `status` is exactly one of: `todo`, `in-progress`, `done`.
- Error contract: `404` missing id; `422` missing title or unknown status. Do not invent other codes.
- Any new endpoint gets a same-layer test before the change is done.

## Conventions

- Match the style of the file you edit; do not reformat untouched lines.
- Keep the three backends behaviourally identical — same request/response shape.
- Prefer editing an existing file over creating a new one.

## Verify

- .NET: `dotnet test` in `backend-dotnet/`
- Python: `pytest` in `backend-python/`
- Java: `./mvnw -B test` in `backend-java/`
- Frontend: `npm test -- --run` in `frontend/`
